        "id": "relative",
        "title": "1. Relative pronouns: agreement and case",
        "practiceTopic": "relative",
        "body": [
          "A relative pronoun links a description to an antecedent. It agrees with that antecedent in gender and number, but its case comes from its role inside the relative clause. In βίον ... ὃς ἡδονὴν ἔχει, βίον is accusative while ὃς is nominative because it is the subject of ἔχει.",
          "First bracket the relative clause, then ask who does what within it. Do not simply copy the antecedent’s case. The relative has a rough breathing and usually an accent: distinguish ὅς from the article ὁ."
        ],
        "table": {
          "title": "1. Relative pronouns: agreement and case",
          "headers": [
