  "culture": {
    "title": "Training for Whose Life?",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-26-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: Socrates and Aristippus discuss education near a palaestra",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction, not an ancient eyewitness image."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet the Memorabilia: an argument with Aristippus",
        "body": [
          "In Memorabilia 2.1, Xenophon’s Socrates questions Aristippus about the education of someone intended to rule. Food, drink, sleep, heat, and cold become tests of whether a person can put a responsibility before an immediate comfort.",
          "Aristippus supplies resistance: he does not simply ask how to become the ruler Socrates imagines. Read his preference for another kind of life as part of the argument. Xenophon presents a crafted exchange, not a neutral survey of all Athenian educational practices."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Education beyond a classroom",
        "body": [
          "The passage places education in the body’s routines as well as in speech. A ruler may have to work when tired or face a difficult environment. These examples make an abstract question—what kind of life should one prepare for?—concrete.",
          "Our ravine and work scene are teaching arrangements. They let relatives identify the person who will rule, the friend who needs help, and the life someone chooses. Do not turn every descriptive sentence into a new historical fact about Aristippus."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Who chooses the training?",
        "body": [
          "A training program has a purpose, a designer, and a person who bears its costs. Socrates starts with the prospective ruler’s obligations; Aristippus challenges the desirability of that role. These are different starting points, not just different levels of effort.",
          "Imagine designing training for a guide responsible for companions on a journey. Explain why each exercise is relevant. Then ask which hardship would add no useful skill. Discipline needs a reason, not merely a reputation for toughness."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Follow the relative, follow the responsibility",
        "body": [
          "Write three English clauses: “the person who leads,” “the friend whom we help,” and “the work that cannot wait.” Decide who is the subject within each clause.",
          "Then locate ὃς, ᾧ, and ἃ in the reading. The case changes because the role changes. Grammar makes the network of responsibilities visible: the leader is not the subject of every action, and the person helped is not necessarily the one giving orders."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "Who challenges Socrates in this passage?",
        "answer": "Aristippus"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What is the opening educational problem?",
        "answer": "how to train someone intended to rule"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why include Aristippus’s preference?",
        "answer": "It questions whether the proposed ruling life is desirable."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What is the status of the lesson’s ravine scene?",
        "answer": "a teaching arrangement"
      }
    ],
    "review": {
      "title": "Before the Final Quiz",
      "items": [
        "Identify the genre, purpose, and adaptation boundary of Memorabilia.",
        "Track each relative pronoun back to its antecedent; its case comes from its role in its own clause.",
        "What is the status of the lesson’s ravine scene? a teaching arrangement",
        "What should justify a training exercise in the activity? its relation to an actual responsibility",
        "What can relative clauses help reveal? different people’s roles in the action"
      ]
    },
