    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "regular-comparison",
        "title": "1. Regular comparatives and superlatives",
        "practiceTopic": "regular-comparison",
        "body": [
          "A comparative sets one item against another; a superlative places it at an extreme within a group. Many adjectives use -τερος and -τατος: σοφός, σοφώτερος, σοφώτατος. Vowel length in the stem affects the connecting vowel; learn the listed forms.",
          "Comparatives and superlatives still agree with their nouns. The ending -τερος does not remove gender, number, or case. A superlative can also have an intensive sense, “very wise,” where no definite comparison group is stated."
        ],
        "table": {
          "title": "1. Regular comparatives and superlatives",
