        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "irregular-comparison",
        "title": "2. Irregular comparison",
        "practiceTopic": "irregular-comparison",
        "body": [
          "Frequent adjectives may change stems. ἀγαθός has βελτίων/βέλτιστος for “better/best”; κακός has χείρων/χείριστος; πολύς has πλείων/πλεῖστος; ὀλίγος has ἐλάττων/ἐλάχιστος. Meaning and context govern the choice among alternative Greek forms.",
          "Comparatives in -ων have masculine/feminine nominative singular -ων and neuter -ον. βελτίονα and βελτίω are alternative accusative singular masculine/feminine forms. Recognize these contracted alternatives without treating them as different degrees."
        ],
        "table": {
          "title": "2. Irregular comparison",
          "headers": [
            "Form or construction",
            "Meaning / analysis"
          ],
          "greekColumns": [
            0
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ἀγαθός → βελτίων → βέλτιστος",
              "good → better → best"
            ],
            [
              "κακός → χείρων → χείριστος",
              "bad → worse → worst"
            ],
            [
              "πολύς → πλείων → πλεῖστος",
              "much/many → more → most"
            ],
            [
              "ὀλίγος → ἐλάττων → ἐλάχιστος",
              "little/few → less/fewer → least/fewest"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "examples": [],
        "checks": [
          {
            "prompt": "Choose “best.”",
            "answer": "βέλτιστος"
          },
          {
            "prompt": "Identify the comparative of πολύς in this table.",
            "answer": "πλείων"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comparison-syntax",
        "title": "3. Than: genitive or ἤ",
        "practiceTopic": "comparison-syntax",
        "body": [
          "Greek can express the second term of comparison with a genitive: σοφώτερος τοῦ φίλου, “wiser than the friend.” Alternatively ἤ introduces the comparison, and the case follows the parallel construction: οὗτος σοφώτερός ἐστιν ἢ ἐκεῖνος.",
          "A superlative often takes a partitive genitive: σοφώτατος τῶν φίλων, “wisest of the friends.” Distinguish this group from the genitive used after a comparative. Do not confuse either with a possessive genitive."
        ],
        "table": {
