      {
        "id": "comparative-adverbs",
        "title": "4. Comparing actions and needs",
        "practiceTopic": "comparative-adverbs",
        "body": [
          "An adverb modifies an action or description rather than agreeing with a noun. Comparative adverbs commonly use the neuter singular comparative adjective; superlative adverbs use the neuter plural superlative: σοφώτερον, σοφώτατα.",
          "In the reading, more possessions do not automatically produce better living. Keep the grammar precise: βέλτιον ζῆν compares ways of living; βελτίων βίος describes a life. μᾶλλον and μάλιστα are useful general adverbs meaning “more” and “most.”"
        ],
