    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Regular comparatives and superlatives: A comparative sets one item against another; a superlative places it at an extreme within a group. Many adjectives use -τερος and -τατος: σοφός, σοφώτερος, σοφώτατος. Vowel length in the stem affects the connecting vowel; learn the listed forms.",
        "2. Irregular comparison: Frequent adjectives may change stems. ἀγαθός has βελτίων/βέλτιστος for “better/best”; κακός has χείρων/χείριστος; πολύς has πλείων/πλεῖστος; ὀλίγος has ἐλάττων/ἐλάχιστος. Meaning and context govern the choice among alternative Greek forms.",
        "3. Than: genitive or ἤ: Greek can express the second term of comparison with a genitive: σοφώτερος τοῦ φίλου, “wiser than the friend.” Alternatively ἤ introduces the comparison, and the case follows the parallel construction: οὗτος σοφώτερός ἐστιν ἢ ἐκεῖνος.",
        "4. Comparing actions and needs: An adverb modifies an action or description rather than agreeing with a noun. Comparative adverbs commonly use the neuter singular comparative adjective; superlative adverbs use the neuter plural superlative: σοφώτερον, σοφώτατα."
      ]
