  "culture": {
    "title": "How Much Is Enough?",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-27-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: Socrates and Critobulus consider household resources",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction, not an ancient eyewitness image."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet Xenophon’s Oeconomicus",
        "body": [
          "The Oeconomicus explores the management of an oikos: household, property, work, and people. Its title gives us a distant ancestor of “economics,” but its subject is not identical to a modern national economy. Socrates talks first with Critobulus; later he recounts a conversation with Ischomachus.",
          "Our selection comes from chapter 2. Socrates provocatively compares his own small resources with Critobulus’s much greater resources and obligations. The dialogue asks whether wealth is measured by an inventory alone or by what a person needs to accomplish."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "An estate is not a bank balance",
        "body": [
          "A household’s resources could include land, equipment, stored produce, and the labor of people with unequal status. Counting possessions leaves out maintenance, obligations, and who controls the work.",
          "The lesson’s resource comparison is deliberately qualitative. We have not converted an ancient estate into a modern salary or assigned an invented market price to the banner’s objects. The point is the relationship between means and demands."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The surprise of the comparison",
        "body": [
          "Socrates’s challenge works because Critobulus seems obviously wealthier. A comparative such as πλουσιώτερος can describe possessions, while a different comparison asks who has enough. Those claims need not have the same answer.",
          "Try a two-column inventory for an imaginary journey: resources on one side, necessary demands on the other. Add one obligation that makes the apparently richer traveler less secure. This is a reasoning exercise, not advice that poverty is good."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Whose “enough” counts?",
        "body": [
          "The household manager’s comfort is not the only perspective. Ask whether the people doing the work have enough rest, food, and security. Xenophon’s household discussion is shaped by ancient inequalities; we can read closely while asking questions its speakers do not fully answer.",
          "Use one comparative, one superlative, and one comparative adverb to explain your judgment: a larger store, the most urgent need, acting more wisely. Keep the three constructions distinct."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "What is the main scope of the Oeconomicus?",
        "answer": "household and estate management"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Who speaks with Socrates in the opening selection?",
        "answer": "Critobulus"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What makes Socrates’s comparison surprising?",
        "answer": "The man with fewer resources may have fewer unmet demands."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why avoid a modern salary conversion here?",
        "answer": "The lesson has no justified equivalent price or income calculation."
      }
    ],
    "review": {
      "title": "Before the Final Quiz",
      "items": [
        "Identify the genre, purpose, and adaptation boundary of Oeconomicus.",
        "Comparatives require a standard of comparison; distinguish possessions from needs.",
        "Why avoid a modern salary conversion here? The lesson has no justified equivalent price or income calculation.",
        "Which perspective broadens the household question? the needs of the people doing the work",
        "What does the inventory activity compare? resources with demands and obligations"
      ]
    },
