    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. Contract verbs in -όω: In -όω verbs, ο contracts with following vowels: δηλόω becomes δηλῶ, δηλόεις becomes δηλοῖς. Present infinitive δηλοῦν comes from δηλόειν. Read the contraction before deciding person and number.",
        "2. Contract second-declension nouns: Contract nouns bring adjacent vowels together in the noun stem and ending. νοῦς, νοῦ, “mind,” and πλοῦς, πλοῦ, “voyage,” preserve familiar second-declension cases under contracted spellings.",
        "3. Numbers and measured claims: One, two, three, and four require special attention. εἷς/μία/ἕν has three genders; τρεῖς is masculine/feminine, τρία neuter; τέτταρες is masculine/feminine, τέτταρα neuter. Most cardinal numbers from five through one hundred are indeclinable.",
        "4. ὡς and its compounds in context: ὡς has several uses; let the sentence determine the sense. With a comparison it can mean “as”; with a superlative, “as ... as possible.” ὥσπερ means “just as,” and ὥστε commonly introduces a result, often with an infinitive."
      ]
    }
