  "culture": {
    "title": "The Household as a System of Work",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-32-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: Women and workers organize equipment in a Greek household",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction, not an ancient eyewitness image."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet the Oeconomicus: Ischomachus’s account",
        "body": [
          "Later in the Oeconomicus, Socrates recounts his conversation with Ischomachus about household management. In chapters 8–9, arrangement and finding things become vivid examples of order. We hear a man describe the household’s practices and his wife’s role through a reported conversation.",
          "That layered voice matters. This is not a diary written by the wife or a complete account from every worker. Our adaptation foregrounds practical knowledge and the labor that makes an orderly household function, while identifying new teaching examples as such."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Order can save a search",
        "body": [
          "A tool placed where people expect to find it reduces wasted effort. Ischomachus uses comparisons, including the compact organization of a ship, to make order seem both useful and impressive. Different things require suitable places.",
          "Make a small plan for a shared work area. Choose three objects and explain why each belongs in its place. Then ask whether another person could understand your arrangement without reading your mind. Order that only its designer understands is fragile."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Who makes order possible?",
        "body": [
          "The household includes people of unequal status and power, including enslaved laborers. Organization does not erase that inequality. A neat room can conceal whose work keeps it neat and who has authority to change the arrangement.",
          "The lesson asks you to notice expertise: someone must know where an object belongs, be able to move it, and have time to return it. Those are distinct conditions. Do not assume that ownership and practical knowledge belong to the same person."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Three verbs, three questions",
        "body": [
          "Use κεῖται to ask where an item lies, δύναται to ask who can retrieve it, and ἐπίσταται to ask who knows how to handle it. Explain a case where a person knows how but lacks the opportunity or permission.",
          "The banner is an educational reconstruction of organized work, not a plan of Ischomachus’s excavated home. Close with one change that would make your imaginary work area easier for everyone who uses it, and state who should help decide."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "Whose household account does Socrates report in this part of the Oeconomicus?",
        "answer": "Ischomachus’s"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why does the narrative’s layered voice matter?",
        "answer": "It is not a direct account from every household member."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What practical benefit can arrangement provide?",
        "answer": "making needed objects easier to find"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What should students notice behind a neat room?",
        "answer": "the labor and unequal authority that sustain it"
      }
    ],
    "review": {
      "title": "Before the Final Quiz",
      "items": [
