        "1. First aorist passive: The first aorist passive uses a stem in -θη- with active-shaped secondary endings: ἐλύθην, ἐλύθης, ἐλύθη. These endings do not make the meaning active. The stem, principal parts, and context identify the passive.",
        "2. Second aorist passive: A second aorist passive has -η- rather than -θη-: γράφω has ἐγράφην, “I was written/inscribed,” and φαίνω has ἐφάνην, often “I appeared.” The label describes formation; some passive-shaped forms have an intransitive meaning.",
        "3. Future passive: The future passive is built on the aorist passive stem with -σ- and middle endings: λυθήσομαι, “I shall be released.” A second-passive stem likewise gives γραφήσομαι. Unlike the aorist indicative, these futures have no augment.",
        "4. Deponents and responsible translation: Deponent verbs do not all form the aorist the same way. ἀποκρίνομαι has ἀπεκρινάμην, “I answered,” with middle aorist forms. βούλομαι has ἐβουλήθην, “I wished,” with passive-shaped aorist forms. Neither requires an English passive."
