  "culture": {
    "title": "Training the Self: Who Chooses the Training?",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-33-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: Spartan boys practice under a supervisor",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction, not an ancient eyewitness image."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet the Constitution of the Lacedaemonians",
        "body": [
          "Xenophon’s Constitution of the Lacedaemonians describes and evaluates Spartan institutions, often attributing their distinctive features to Lycurgus. “Constitution” here means an account of a community’s arrangements and way of life, not a modern written constitution with numbered rights.",
          "Chapter 2 discusses boys’ education and contrasts Spartan practices with those of other Greeks. Xenophon often admires the system. His admiration is part of the evidence we must interpret; it does not make the account an impartial description of every child’s experience."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "A public supervisor, a disciplined body",
        "body": [
          "The paidonomos, a public supervisor of boys, has authority in Xenophon’s account. Bare feet, limited clothing, and controlled food supply form part of a program intended to cultivate endurance and resourcefulness.",
          "The passage also includes taking food secretly and punishment when caught. Describe that unsettling detail accurately before evaluating it. The lesson does not turn punishment or deprivation into a model for students to imitate."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The missing viewpoint",
        "body": [
          "A program can look successful from the viewpoint of the authority directing it while imposing costs on those undergoing it. The boys’ voices are not represented in the same way as Xenophon’s explanation of the institutions.",
          "Ask what additional evidence you would want: experiences of participants, outcomes over time, or comparison with alternatives. State which question the passage can help answer and which it cannot answer by itself."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Put the agent back in",
        "body": [
          "Change “the boys were trained” into an active sentence naming the supervisor. Then ask who selected the goals and who had power to refuse. The passive is useful, but it can make authority less visible.",
          "Design one imaginary training activity with a clear skill goal and a stopping rule. Explain how its usefulness would be judged. This is a modern discussion exercise, not a proposed reconstruction of an ancient Spartan regulation."
        ]
      }
    ],
