    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "1. The genitive absolute: A genitive absolute supplies a circumstance with its own subject and participle, both in the genitive. τοῦ Σωκράτους λέγοντος means “while Socrates was speaking.” The phrase stands grammatically apart from the main clause: οἱ φίλοι ἤκουον, “the friends listened.”",
        "2. Participial aspect and shared subjects: A present participle normally shows action simultaneous with the main verb; an aorist participle normally presents a prior action. Thus λέγοντος may mean “while speaking,” and παυσαμένου “after it had ended.” The surrounding tense supplies the time frame.",
        "3. ἵστημι: make stand or stand?: Present active ἵστημι is normally transitive, “make stand, set up.” Its first aorist ἔστησα also causes someone or something to stand. The second aorist ἔστην is intransitive, “I stood.” This difference is lexical and grammatical, not merely a change of time.",
        "4. καθίστημι and ἀφίστημι: Compounds retain the important causative/intransitive contrast. καθίστημι can mean “appoint” someone to a role; κατέστησα means “I appointed,” while κατέστην can mean “I became/was established.” An appointment often uses two accusatives: the person and the role."
      ]
    }
  },
