    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet the Memorabilia: definitions tested by conduct",
        "body": [
          "This lesson joins Memorabilia 3.9.4–5 with 4.5.1–12. In the first, Xenophon relates wisdom and self-control to knowing and doing what is good. In the second, Socrates examines the freedom lost when appetites govern conduct. These passages are related arguments, not one continuous ancient scene.",
          "Across the module, the Memorabilia has offered habits, dialogue, allegory, and examination. Its variety serves Xenophon’s defense of Socrates as a useful teacher. You can now ask not only “What does Socrates say?” but also “How does Xenophon make this portrait persuasive?”"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Freedom as a capacity",
        "body": [
          "In the argument, self-command makes learning, deliberating, and helping possible. Restraint is not merely the absence of visible movement. A person may sit quietly while being unable to attend to what matters.",
          "Try the contrast with an ordinary task: putting off an interruption long enough to listen to a friend. What valuable action does restraint enable? Name that action rather than treating self-denial as its own reward."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "A metaphor with a social cost",
        "body": [
          "Socrates’s language of being enslaved to pleasure draws force from a world where actual slavery existed. A metaphor of inner servitude should not make actual coerced labor disappear from view.",
          "Distinguish the philosophical comparison from legal and social status. Someone can be legally enslaved regardless of personal self-control. Conversely, the argument can criticize the conduct of a legally free person. The two senses are not interchangeable."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Build your definition with evidence",
        "body": [
          "Choose two readings from the module that complicate one another: Socrates’s simple meal and the hungry army, or household order and the captive’s vulnerability. Write a definition of self-control broad enough to address both.",
          "Attach one piece of evidence to each part of the definition. Then use a genitive absolute to set a circumstance and a finite verb to name a responsible action. Your final test is whether the definition guides conduct, not whether it sounds severe."
        ]
      }
