  "culture": {
    "title": "The Disciplined Life: From Self to City",
    "banner": {
      "image": "assets/lesson-36-banner.png",
      "alt": "Educational reconstruction: Athenian citizens cautiously return toward shared civic life",
      "caption": "Modern educational reconstruction, not an ancient eyewitness image."
    },
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Meet Xenophon’s Hellenica",
        "body": [
          "The Hellenica narrates Greek affairs from the closing years of the Peloponnesian War into the fourth century BCE. It is political and military history, with speeches, choices, omissions, and judgments that invite scrutiny. It differs in purpose and scale from the Socratic dialogues and from the literary Cyrus narrative.",
          "Our passage, 2.4.38–43, concerns reconciliation after civil conflict at Athens. Keep its sequence clear: settlement and return in 403 BCE, sacrifice and renewed civic life, then a later episode involving Eleusis. Our supplementary-participle examples are teaching constructions, not additional testimony."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Return with arms, then a shared city",
        "body": [
          "The men from Piraeus return armed to the city and sacrifice to Athena on the Acropolis. A political settlement must become a way of living together after violence. Former opponents do not suddenly lose their memories.",
          "The oath not to bear grudges concerns how old wrongs will be used in renewed civic relations. It is not a claim that nothing happened. The settlement’s restrictions and exceptions should not be reduced to the slogan “everyone forgot everything.”"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Do not crop out Eleusis",
        "body": [
          "Section 43 reports a later confrontation: leaders from Eleusis are killed when they come for a conference, and further reconciliation follows through friends and relatives. This disturbing event limits any simple picture of an effortless, wholly peaceful restoration.",
          "Chronology matters ethically as well as historically. If we merge the later killing into the first return, we misdescribe the sequence; if we omit it, we make reconciliation look easier and cleaner than Xenophon’s own account allows."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "A citizen’s restraint",
        "body": [
          "Stage a discussion without reenacting violence. One speaker explains why a community needs rules against renewed retaliation; another asks how victims’ losses can be acknowledged. Require each to state a concern the other has correctly identified.",
          "Then trace the module’s movement: governing appetite, recognizing bodily limits, managing power over others, and living with former opponents. Use a supplementary participle to describe stopping an action. Explain what constructive action must follow the stopping."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "What kind of work is the Hellenica?",
        "answer": "political and military history of Greek affairs"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What is the date of the initial return and restoration discussed here?",
        "answer": "403 BCE"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Where do the returning men sacrifice to Athena?",
        "answer": "the Acropolis"
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Does the oath mean that no wrongs occurred or that everyone literally forgot them?",
        "answer": "No; it concerns renewed relations and retaliation after real harms."
      }
    ],
    "review": {
      "title": "Before the Final Quiz",
      "items": [
        "Identify the genre, purpose, and adaptation boundary of Hellenica.",
        "Read supplementary participles as completing verbs of perception or continuance; bring together the module’s patterns without introducing advanced indirect discourse.",
        "Does the oath mean that no wrongs occurred or that everyone literally forgot them? No; it concerns renewed relations and retaliation after real harms.",
        "How does section 43 relate to the initial return? It reports a later Eleusis episode.",
        "Why retain the killing of the Eleusinian leaders in the discussion? It complicates a simple story of peaceful reconciliation."
      ]
    },
